[Music]  for people living with type 1 diabetes  tight glucose levels are really  important because they dramatically  reduce the risk of complications  affecting the eyes the nerves and the  kidneys  the artificial pancreas offers great  promise for patients with diabetes  but researchers are still in the process  of gathering evidence through trials  researchers like helen murphy at the  university of east anglia  so the importance of tight glucose  levels is well established but actually  the realities of how to achieve it are  really quite challenging so for those of  us with a normal functioning pancreas  our bodies are making insulin  and adjusting that insulin really every  five to ten minutes  whereas for people with diabetes they're  having to inject insulin  they might do that three or five times a  day  and but it's really difficult to mimic  what the normal functioning pancreas  does  so really people living with type 1  diabetes are almost need to be expert  nutritionists  they need to understand exactly how much  carbohydrates in their food  and then they need to be mathematical  geniuses to match the insulin  dose very precisely to the amount of  carbohydrates that they eat  so the real difference between the  conventional  insulin delivery that's delivered by  injections or by an insulin pump  compared to the artificial pancreas is  that the artificial pancreas is able to  deliver small amounts of insulin  little and often so it's mimicking a  normal functioning pancreas in that way  if you like  because it's adjusting insulin every 10  to 12 minutes including during the  overnight when people are asleep  so the artificial pancreas has got three  components a continuous glucose monitor  an insulin pump and a mathematical  algorithm that links the insulin pump  delivery to the real-time glucose levels  over the last decade the advancing  continuous glucose monitoring has meant  that we've been able to start some high  quality research studies  into the artificial pancreas across a  whole range of different patient groups  so the early studies for the artificial  pancreas have been conducted  in children as young as one and right up  to adults in the age of 80.  now the group that i work with in  particular are pregnant women  so we've been really delighted to be  able to work together  with bioengineers and bring the latest  artificial pancreas developments  to this important patient population  helen has been running a trial of a  newly developed closed-loop artificial  pancreas system at her local hospital  with pregnant women with diabetes the  trial is ongoing  but she spoke to one of the trial  participants specialist midwife nina  willa to hear how it had affected her  glucose control  during her pregnancy well in my previous  pregnancy i was using  a combination of finger pricking and  freestyle libre  and so for this having the alarm  function just on the dexcom alone  is quite life-changing because i don't  have to worry so much about having  significant high pose  but also when my blood sugars rise  um even if i had no awareness of it if i  was sleeping through it  then i wake up always on a perfect blood  sugar level no matter what happens  the um the algorithm just kicks in and  takes care of it  and also i feel more in tune with what's  going on with my body  and more educated and i think that's  been one of the real things we've  noticed the feedback that actually just  the importance of waking up  with a blood sugar that's already on  target rather than  having to spend half of your morning  catching up starting the day  right makes such a difference and i feel  like  i spent a lot of my awake hours and the  sleep  hours worrying about my diabetes when i  wasn't pregnant but then again when  you're pregnant you've got to worry  about the baby as well  it's quite a high stress situation  whereas this  i'm still thinking about it and i'm  still informed but i'm somewhat on  autopilot  and it just reduces my stress levels so  much and really that's our hope for all  pregnant women  that they can be a little bit more on  autopilot with their diabetes  and yeah enjoying a healthy pregnancy  and  delivering safe healthy babies where  we're up to  now is we're i think getting the real  world evidence  that the closely bart visual pancreas  can achieve  tighter glucose levels than any of the  compo individual components by  themselves  so we're up to the larger scale what we  call the phase three or the clinical  effectiveness trials  the next phase of course is going to  involve health economics and regulators  and getting these devices approved for  real-world use and by people with  diabetes  so what i think we're going to see over  the next five to 10 years  will be a wider variety of different  sorts of closed loop artificial pancreas  systems  hopefully we will see pumps that a whole  range of pumps that are  insulin pumps that are compatible with  the artificial pancreas  we'll start to see smaller even more  accurate continuous glucose monitoring  systems and already we see the control  algorithms are  worn on used on a smartphone  people can access the glucose data on a  smart watch  so the devices are becoming less  intrusive more user-friendly  easier to incorporate into normal daily  living  but of course the question everyone  wants to ask is will are these a cure  and you know the honest answer is that  probably they're not don't necessarily  represent a cure they represent  you know fantastic technological  progress  towards a cure we think of it like the  bridge to a cure  so in the meantime the artificial  pancreas is certainly the best  technological solution we have  that's likely to help the largest number  of people achieve the target glucose  levels that they need  to reduce their risk of complications  you

